DERIVED WORK

The USB board drivers here are this module's own work rather than a port of an
existing driver. Where an existing free driver covers the same silicon it is
named below, because the register-level behaviour necessarily agrees with it and
because parts of this module are derived from it.

px4_drv by nns779, GPL-2.0-only
    https://github.com/nns779/px4_drv
    internal/coaxd/usb/board/{plex,px4,pxmlt,isdb2056,s1ur}
    internal/coaxd/usb/bridge/it930x
    internal/coaxd/demod/{cxd2856er,tc90522}
    internal/coaxd/tuner/{r850,rt710,cxd2858er}

The Linux media subsystem's Siano driver, GPL-2.0-or-later
    internal/coaxd/usb/bridge/siano
    internal/coaxd/usb/board/s270

The Linux media subsystem's TDA18271 driver by Michael Krufky, GPL-2.0-or-later
    internal/coaxd/tuner/tda18211/maps.go

    The TDA18211 is the ISDB-T sibling of the TDA18271. The frequency maps that
    file holds -- the RF band, gain taper, bandpass, K/M, image-rejection and
    the two PLL maps -- are the ones tda18271-maps.c carries for the same
    silicon. Nothing else in that package comes from it.

The PX-W3U3 series NM120 tuner driver by Budi Rachmanto, GPL-2.0-only
    internal/coaxd/tuner/nm120

TurboSight's linux_media tree, GPL-2.0-or-later and GPL-2.0-only by file
    https://github.com/tbsdtv/linux_media
    internal/coaxd/demod/{avl6882,cxd2857er,cxd2878,m88rs6060,mxl58x,si2183,sony,stid135,stv091x,tas2101}
    internal/coaxd/tuner/{av201x,r848,rda5816,stv6120}
    internal/coaxd/usb/board/{tbs5520,tbs5520se,tbs5530}
    internal/coaxd/usb/bridge/{tbs,tbs5520,tbs5520se,tbs5530}

    That tree carries each driver under its own terms, and the files here follow
    whichever applies. The copyright lines at the top of each file name the
    holders; grouped by the driver they come from:

      avl6882.c, tas2101.c, av201x.c, r848.c, si2183.c, GPL-2.0-or-later
          Luis Alves, with Rafael Micro's part in r848
      cxd2878.c, m88rs6060.c, GPL-2.0-or-later
          Davin Zhang of TurboSight, over Sony's and Montage's own material
      rda5816.c, MODULE_LICENSE("GPL"), which the kernel reads as v2 or later
          Davin
      mxl58x.c, stv091x.c, stv6120.c, GPL-2.0-only
          Ralph Metzler and Marcus Metzler of Digital Devices, with MaxLinear's
          part in mxl58x
      stid135, GPL-2.0-only over a BSD-3-Clause core
          CrazyCat wrote the frontend; the low-level layer under it is
          STMicroelectronics' Oxford LLA, whose notice is reproduced below
      tbs5520.c, tbs5520se.c, tbs5530.c, GPL-2.0-only
          Konstantin Dimitrov and Davin

The Linux media subsystem's ISDB tuners and demodulator by Akihiro Tsukada,
GPL-2.0-only
    https://git.linuxtv.org/media.git
    internal/coaxd/demod/tc90522
    internal/coaxd/tuner/{mxl301rf,qm1d1b0004,qm1d1c0042}

The Linux media subsystem's DiBcom DiB8000, DiB0090 and dibx000 drivers,
GPL-2.0-only
    https://git.linuxtv.org/media.git
    internal/coaxd/demod/dib8096

    dib8000.c, dibx000_common.c and dib0700_devices.c are GPL-2.0-only;
    dib0090.c is GPL-2.0-or-later and is taken here under version 2. The
    copyright is DiBcom's, with Patrick Boettcher and Olivier Grenie as
    authors. The board values are dib0700_devices.c's STK8096GP entries.

The Linux media subsystem's FC0012 tuner by Hans-Frieder Vogt, GPL-2.0-or-later
    internal/coaxd/tuner/fc0012

The Linux media subsystem's simple PLL driver, GPL-2.0-or-later
    internal/coaxd/tuner/tda6651

    dvb-pll.c by Gerd Knorr and others. The board figures here -- the band
    table, the power-up bytes, the intermediate frequency and the step -- are
    its dvb_pll_tda665x_earth_pt1 entry, which is the TDA6651 as an Earthsoft
    PT1 or PT2 wires it.

The MyGica T230C implementation was developed with reference to the behaviour of
the Linux media subsystem's dvbsky, Si2168 and Si2157/Si2141 drivers. No Linux
kernel driver source or firmware is bundled with this product.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LIBRARIES LOADED AT RUN TIME

libusb-1.0, LGPL-2.1-or-later
    Used by the Windows and macOS builds, and neither bundled nor linked: both
    resolve it by name at run time, so no copy of it forms part of this work.
    The Linux build reaches USB devices through usbfs and does not use libusb
    at all.

